- M1.9 BLOCKER (RESOLVED — see above; original text kept for the record) — 4 findings from the pre-implementation auth state-machine audit (§20 rule 20).** A full 14-attribute review of every authentication path against Revision 15 verified 17 of 21 paths and found 4 defects needing a Document Owner decision. **F1:** TD-12 mandates `POST /auth/refresh` as the sole cookie-authenticated route, but TD-3.1 does not list it, and §3.1 requires CI to fail on any endpoint absent from TD-3 — the mandated endpoint fails the mandated gate (currently carried in `scripts/ci/td3-routes.txt` on TD-12's authority, a workaround standing in for a spec fix). **F2:** TD-12 requires refresh rotation, a server-side revocation list, immediate revocation on suspension, and a 10-second previous-token grace window — all server-side state — but §7 defines no entity to hold any of it; logout therefore cannot invalidate a 30-day refresh token. Same class as the `pre_provisioned_email` gap closed by R15. **F3:** `Rejected` is terminal in TD-1 so the account persists and can still authenticate, yet §4.1b step 4a enumerates only Active/Pending/Suspended-or-deactivated and no screen exists in §2.1 or §14.1 — falling through to a dashboard would be an access-control failure. **F4:** TD-12/§4.3 rely on family-link revocation taking effect immediately, but TD-1 makes `Approved` terminal, TD-2 grants only approve/reject, and §5.6 offers no revoke action — the middleware is defined but the revoked state is unreachable (surfaces at M2). Non-blocking and resolved by reading adjacent clauses, recorded for transparency: **F5** PKCE verifier storage is unspecified (proceeding with a short-lived signed HttpOnly cookie — flow state, not authentication); **F6** step 4a routes on `account_status` without naming `deleted_at`, settled by §4.1's deactivated-account rule; **F7** an Active account with no role lands on §14.4's `NoPermissionState`. Review published for the Owner. **F1+F2 block the session layer, F3 blocks callback routing — implementation stopped before M1.9.**
- ~~OPEN SRS CONTRADICTION (deleted-account login routing)~~ **RESOLVED by Revision 20, option (a)** (Document Owner, 2026-07-25): the `deleted_at` scoping is removed from the lookup and refusal is solely step 4a's job. A soft-deleted person cannot register again without staff action. Test un-skipped and green. Original finding retained below.
- OPEN SRS CONTRADICTION (RESOLVED — see above; original text kept for the record). Surfaced by testing, in text I authored under Revision 15. §7 says the pre-provision fallback "is scoped to `deleted_at IS NULL`" **and, in the same sentence,** that "a suspended or **deleted** account yields the 'Account deactivated' screen" — but the scoping is exactly what makes a deleted account unreachable by that lookup, so the second clause cannot happen. §4.1 separately states that soft-deleted accounts "show 'Account deactivated' … on login attempt. **Never silently re-register or reactivate.**" **Where they disagree:** a soft-deleted account that was **never bound** to a Google identity. Following §7's scoping it is invisible to both lookups and falls through to onboarding — i.e. the platform invites the person to register again, which §4.1's "never silently re-register" appears to forbid; following §4.1 it must reach the deactivated screen, which §7's scoping prevents. **The bound-then-deleted case is NOT ambiguous and is implemented**: the identity lookup now returns deactivated identities flagged rather than hidden, so the account is *found and refused* instead of falling through — hiding it would have invited re-registration, and the DB would then have refused the duplicate `(provider, provider_subject_id)` with an unfriendly 409 anyway. Candidate resolutions: **(a)** drop `deleted_at IS NULL` from the §7 fallback and let `routeByStatus` refuse deleted accounts (one rule, §4.1 satisfied, a deleted person can never re-register without staff action); **(b)** keep the scoping and state in §4.1 that a never-bound deleted account may register afresh, since the new account still enters `Pending` and needs approval, so nothing is silently *reactivated*. The integration test for this case is present but `it.skip`ped with the reason inline, so it is visible rather than quietly dropped. **Not resolved by invention.**
- ~~OPEN SRS CONTRADICTION (TD-3 seed vs canonical)~~ **RESOLVED by Revision 21, option (a)** (Document Owner, 2026-07-25): TD-3 is the normative registry for the documented milestones; later milestones add endpoints by SRS revision. CI hard-fails on contradicting, undocumented, or uncited endpoints and reports documented-but-unimplemented ones as PENDING. Branch/Room CRUD unblocked. Original finding retained below.
- OPEN SRS CONTRADICTION (RESOLVED — see above; original text kept for the record). TD-3's own preamble calls its list "**Representative critical operations**" and says "this registry is the **seed** for it [the OpenAPI doc]" — i.e. the registry is a partial seed and the generated OpenAPI document is the binding contract, so OpenAPI ⊇ registry. **§3.1 (Revision 10) says the opposite:** the registry "is the **canonical** API contract — it defines which endpoints exist", and "CI fails if the generated document contains an endpoint **absent from TD-3** or omits a TD-3 endpoint", i.e. OpenAPI == registry. **Where it bites now:** §5.6, §14.1 and §14.2 all require a Branches & Rooms admin screen with CRUD, but TD-3 lists **only** `POST /admin/branches/{id}/event-backfill` — no branch or room CRUD routes at all. Under §3.1 those endpoints are "invented" and hard-fail CI; under TD-3's preamble they are the expected growth of a seed. The same applies to every CRUD screen in M2–M6 (users, groups, levels, taxonomy, content, calendar), so this is not a one-off. Candidate resolutions: **(a)** treat TD-3 as the **seed** its preamble says it is — CI keeps hard-failing only on endpoints that contradict TD-3's *semantics*, and §3.1's "absent from TD-3" clause is narrowed to the routes TD-3 does define; **(b)** treat TD-3 as **exhaustive** and extend it, by SRS revision, with every CRUD route each milestone needs (largest spec churn, strongest control); **(c)** something else. **Not resolved by invention** — under either reading I would be adding endpoints to a contract, which §20 rule 16 makes an Owner decision. Everything else in M1 proceeds.
- **OPEN AMBIGUITY 3 (recorded per Document Owner instruction; decide before production) — `SUPER_ADMIN_EMAIL` reseeding.** The §15.1 Super Admin seed is idempotent **per email address**, not "exactly one Super Admin". Changing `SUPER_ADMIN_EMAIL` and re-running the seed therefore **creates a second active Super Admin and leaves the previous row active and unclaimed**, rather than retiring it. Observed on 2026-07-25 when the Owner switched the value: `admin@example.com` remains `active` alongside the real address. Harmless in development (nobody owns the stale address), but in production an orphaned, permanently-unclaimable Super Admin row is a standing access-control artefact. §15.1 does not say which behaviour it wants — retire the superseded row, refuse to seed a second, or accept multiple Super Admins by design — so **no behaviour has been invented**. Options to decide before the first production seed: (a) keep the value stable from the first production seed and treat a change as a manual operation; (b) have the seed deactivate any Super Admin row whose `pre_provisioned_email` no longer matches the env var; (c) accept multiple Super Admins as legitimate and document it.
- SRS REVISION IN FORCE: Revision 21 (2026-07-25, Document Owner approved) — see SRS-R21 row below.
