| 2026-07-26 | M2.1b | **Token-consumption invariant confirmed, stated explicitly, and pinned by a retry test (Document Owner request).** Verified already guaranteed: the `jti` insert sits inside `prisma.$transaction` and is the FIRST write, so any later failure rolls it back with everything else. The SRS states the mechanism (§4.1b "the `jti` is inserted … inside the registration transaction"; TD-4.1 "the token-consumption insert inside this transaction is the replay guard") but **never spells out the retry consequence** — 0 matches for it — so per the Owner's "implementation or SRS note" authorisation it is recorded as a **binding implementation note** in `registration.service.ts` and **no SRS change was made**. The invariant as stated: the token is consumed only as part of the transaction creating every registration row; a rollback un-consumes it so the applicant can retry; no path permanently consumes it unless the registration commits. **New RETRY test proves it from the applicant's side rather than by inspecting a row:** a blocking identity forces attempt 1 to fail at the final write (`DUPLICATE`, `jti` row absent), the transient cause is removed as an admin merging a duplicate would do, **the same token then succeeds**, the `jti` is spent only at that point, and a third attempt is a genuine `STATE_CONFLICT` replay. The failure this prevents is silent and severe: a token burned by a half-failed attempt would leave an applicant holding a dead single-use credential with no account and no way to get another short of restarting the whole Google flow, since §4.1b issues exactly one token per callback. 48/48 integration tests green | Invariant documented and enforced by test, not by convention | None |
